BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ottawa-school-of-art-exhibition-proposals-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ottawa School of Art: Call for Exhibition Proposals 2027-
 2028
DESCRIPTION:The Ottawa School of Art invites artists and collectives to sub
 mit exhibition proposals for its 2027-2028 season\, welcoming current and 
 proposed work across all media including painting\, sculpture\, photograph
 y\, installation and video. Group exhibition proposals are accepted. Succe
 ssful applicants exhibit at ONE campus gallery\, but proposals may be subm
 itted for either or both venues\; an application to both must demonstrate 
 a clear understanding of how the exhibition would work best within each ga
 llery's dimensions (floor plans are published with the call). VENUES: the 
 J.W. Stellick Gallery at the ByWard Market campus\, dedicated to giving OS
 A students and the community a wide variety of contemporary art\, and the 
 OSA Orleans Gallery on the Centrum Level of the Shenkman Arts Centre. Arti
 sts receive an Ontario CARFAC exhibition fee. SELECTION: a jury of three a
 rt professionals selects exhibitions on the quality of the work\, the inte
 grity of the proposal\, and its relation to OSA's programs. SUBMISSION: al
 l six components are required\, incomplete proposals are withdrawn without
  notification\, and poorly organised proposals will not be reviewed. (1) C
 ompleted application cover form with contact information and exhibition de
 tails. (2) A written exhibition proposal outlining the concept and intent 
 of the proposed or current body of work. (3) One artist statement per prop
 osal\; group proposals submit a single collective statement\, not individu
 al statements. (4) An up-to-date CV for the exhibiting artist\, or a CV fo
 r each participating artist in a group proposal. (5) Up to 10 visual files
  of work completed within the last two years. An artwork and a detail of t
 he same artwork count as two images\; images must be JPG only\, 300 dpi\, 
 no larger than 2MB each\, RGB\, maximum 1240 pixels on the longest side. E
 ach video counts as one file\, total video time per proposal must not exce
 ed 15 minutes\, and videos must be AVI\, MOV or MP4 and no larger than 500
 MB each. Precede each file name with a zero-padded number and the title\, 
 for example 01Artwork title. (6) A corresponding numbered visual file list
  with title\, year\, medium and dimensions (H x W x D)\, specifying inches
  or centimetres. FILES MUST BE SENT VIA WETRANSFER to boutique@artottawa.c
 a\; proposals submitted by post\, hand delivery or email will not be accep
 ted. Deadline 4:30 pm ET\, Friday 4 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Ottaw
 a School of Art (OSA)\nLocation: Ottawa\, Ontario\, Canada. Two venues: th
 e J.W. Stellick Gallery at the ByWard Market campus\, and the OSA Orleans 
 Gallery on the Centrum Level of the Shenkman Arts Centre.\nAward: Artists 
 receive an Ontario CARFAC exhibition fee (the amount is not stated in the 
 call and follows the CARFAC schedule). No application fee.\nDuration: One 
 exhibition slot in the 2027-2028 exhibition season at one campus gallery.\
 n\nMore: https://www.artottawa.ca/call-for-exhibition-proposals?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.artottawa.ca/call-for-exhibition-proposals?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ottawa\, Ontario\, Canada. Two venues: the J.W. Stellick Gallery a
 t the ByWard Market campus\, and the OSA Orleans Gallery on the Centrum Le
 vel of the Shenkman Arts Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:designto-yonge-st-clair-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Call for Proposals: Activate Yonge + St. Clair During Des
 ignTO Festival 2027 (Toronto)
DESCRIPTION:Artists\, architects\, designers\, fabricators\, and multidisci
 plinary creatives are invited to submit proposals for a series of site-spe
 cific installations at Yonge + St. Clair as part of DesignTO Festival 2027
 . For more than seven years the Yonge + St. Clair BIA has partnered with D
 esignTO to transform publicly accessible spaces throughout the neighbourho
 od into an exhibition of art\, design\, and placemaking. For the 2027 Fest
 ival\, SEVEN installation opportunities are available across lobbies\, gal
 leries\, and window spaces: 2 St. Clair Ave W (Lobby)\, 2 St. Clair Ave E 
 (Lower Level Window)\, 30 St. Clair Ave W (Lobby)\, 40 St. Clair Ave W (Lo
 bby)\, 55 St. Clair Ave W (Lobby)\, 95 St. Clair Ave W (Gallery)\, and 152
 1 Yonge St (Window). One installation will be commissioned per location. T
 HEME: 'Intersections' - proposals may explore convergence\, transformation
 \, movement\, identity\, exchange\, and the evolving relationship between 
 people and place. Artists may submit proposals for multiple locations\, bu
 t each proposal must be tailored to its site and submitted separately\; on
 ly one proposal per artist or team may be selected. WHO SHOULD APPLY: emer
 ging and established artists\, designers\, architects\, fabricators\, and 
 multidisciplinary creative practitioners working in any medium\; preferenc
 e given to artists based in Toronto and the Greater Toronto Area. BUDGET: 
 selected projects receive $5\,000 to $7\,500 CAD inclusive of artist fees\
 , materials\, fabrication\, transportation\, installation\, and de-install
 ation. Artists are responsible for the fabrication\, transportation\, inst
 allation\, and de-installation of their work. KEY DATES: submission deadli
 ne 13 September 2026 at 5:00pm ET\; selected artists notified late Septemb
 er 2026\; final project information due October 2026\; installation period
  14 to 21 January 2027\; DesignTO Festival exhibition 22 to 31 January 202
 7\; de-installation 1 to 2 February 2027. HOW TO APPLY: submit a single PD
 F (artist information and biography\, project description and executive su
 mmary\, visual materials\, detailed line-item budget\, and current resume)
  via email or Dropbox link to info@yongestclair.ca with the subject line '
 DesignTO Festival 2027 - Installation Name - Artist Name'. Free to apply.\
 n\nOrganisation: DesignTO\nLocation: Yonge + St. Clair\, Toronto\, Canada.
  Preference given to artists based in Toronto and the Greater Toronto Area
 .\nAward: Between $5\,000 and $7\,500 CAD per selected project\, inclusive
  of artist fees\, materials\, fabrication\, transportation\, installation\
 , and de-installation. Free to apply.\nDuration: Installation presented du
 ring DesignTO Festival 2027\, 22 to 31 January 2027 (install 14 to 21 Janu
 ary\, de-install 1 to 2 February)\n\nMore: https://designto.org/blog/call-
 for-proposals-activate-yonge-st-clair-during-designto-festival-2027/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://designto.org/blog/call-for-proposals-activate-yonge-st-clair-du
 ring-designto-festival-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Yonge + St. Clair\, Toronto\, Canada. Preference given to artists 
 based in Toronto and the Greater Toronto Area.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canada-council-circulation-touring-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261007
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261008
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canada Council for the Arts: Arts Across Canada and Abroa
 d\, Circulation and Touring (October 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:Funds the circulation and touring of artists\, exhibitions and 
 artistic works by artists\, groups and organisations from Canada\, nationa
 lly and internationally. Media arts\, digital arts and new media\, inter-a
 rts\, and music and sound art are all explicitly named as supported practi
 ces\, so experimental sound and creative technology work is in scope. This
  component sits inside Arts Across Canada and Abroad\, the merged programm
 e introduced in 2025 that replaced the separate Arts Across Canada and Art
 s Abroad programmes\; the old Digital Now programme is discontinued. IMPOR
 TANT AND UNDERUSED: non-Canadian organisations are eligible via the Foreig
 n arts festival\, biennale or book fair profile\, defined as an incorporat
 ed organisation headquartered outside Canada running an international medi
 a arts festival\, an international literary or storytelling festival\, an 
 international book fair\, or a major international visual arts biennial\, 
 triennial or quinquennial. That support is restricted to presenting Canadi
 an media\, literary or visual artists and their work\, and it is not adver
 tised on the programme page. For digital and media arts\, requests must be
  for work independent of the commercial film\, television and video game i
 ndustries\, and only the creator or director may apply\, not producers or 
 production companies. Deadlines run three times a year. The sister compone
 nt Representation and Promotion has continuous rolling intake and the same
  foreign-festival eligibility.\n\nOrganisation: Canada Council for the Art
 s\nLocation: Touring and circulation within Canada and internationally.\nA
 ward: CAD. Minimum and maximum are NOT published publicly\; amounts are di
 sclosed only inside the Council's client portal at arts-services.ca\, whic
 h requires a login. No application fee. Council funds cannot cover expense
 s already covered by another revenue source\, and combined municipal\, pro
 vincial and federal grants must demonstrate complementarity of public supp
 ort.\nDuration: Project-based\; results usually notified within 5 months o
 f the deadline.\n\nMore: https://canadacouncil.ca/funding/grants/arts-acro
 ss-canada-and-abroad?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://canadacouncil.ca/funding/grants/arts-across-canada-and-abroad?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Touring and circulation within Canada and internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:canada-council-public-outreach-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261015
SUMMARY:Deadline: Canada Council for the Arts: Arts Across Canada and Abroa
 d\, Public Outreach (October 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:Funds activities that build connections between artists and div
 erse audiences and contribute to public appreciation and enjoyment of the 
 arts in Canada. This is the closest Canada Council fit for running a publi
 c programme\, a single festival edition\, a talks series or audience-facin
 g exhibition programming\, as distinct from creating the artwork itself. I
 t is open to arts festivals\, presenters and event organisers under the Pr
 esenting organization profile\, alongside Artistic organizations\, Artisti
 c groups and Arts supporting groups\, and the profile explicitly includes 
 visual arts festivals\, biennials\, triennials and quinquennials. Digital 
 arts and new media\, media arts\, inter-arts\, and music and sound art are
  named among funded practices\, so experimental sound art and creative tec
 hnology programming qualify. IMPORTANT: unlike the Circulation and Touring
  component\, international organisations are NOT eligible for Public Outre
 ach. The Council will not fund projects whose primary focus is educational
 \, therapeutic\, recreational or social service delivery\, nor fundraising
  activities or prize and award ceremonies. Deadlines fall twice a year. Th
 e related Composite Activities component offers up to 3 years of support f
 or bundled multi-project programmes and may suit a recurring festival arc 
 better.\n\nOrganisation: Canada Council for the Arts\nLocation: Activities
  must build audiences and public appreciation of the arts in Canada. Organ
 isations must be incorporated or registered in Canada.\nAward: CAD. Minimu
 m and maximum are NOT published publicly\; disclosed only in the client po
 rtal at arts-services.ca\, which requires a login. No application fee. Cou
 ncil funds cannot cover expenses already covered by another revenue source
 \; combined public grants must demonstrate complementarity.\nDuration: Pro
 ject-based\; results usually notified within 5 months of the deadline.\n\n
 More: https://canadacouncil.ca/funding/grants/arts-across-canada-and-abroa
 d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://canadacouncil.ca/funding/grants/arts-across-canada-and-abroad?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities must build audiences and public appreciation of the art
 s in Canada. Organisations must be incorporated or registered in Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:al-purdy-aframe-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: Al Purdy A-frame Association: Al and Eurithe Purdy Reside
 ncy 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Al Purdy A-frame Residency offers Canadian writers time and
  space to work at the historic A-frame home built by poet Al Purdy and his
  wife Eurithe on Roblin Lake in Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Onta
 rio. Poetry is prioritized\, but all writers are welcome. SUPPORT: CAD 750
  per week stipend and paid return travel (subject to the Association's fun
 draising and Canada Council matching)\; residencies run 3 to 12 weeks. ELI
 GIBILITY: Canadian citizens or permanent residents only. DEADLINE: 18 Sept
 ember 2026 (postmark\, or 4:00pm PT for electronic submissions). Apply via
  the residency page.\n\nOrganisation: The Al Purdy A-frame Association\nLo
 cation: Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Ontario\, Canada. Canadian c
 itizens or permanent residents only.\nAward: CAD 750 per week stipend plus
  paid return travel to Ameliasburgh (funding subject to the Association's 
 fundraising and Canada Council matching). Terms of 3 to 12 weeks. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: 3 to 12 weeks\n\nMore: https://www.alpurdy.ca/resid
 ency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.alpurdy.ca/residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ameliasburgh\, Prince Edward County\, Ontario\, Canada. Canadian c
 itizens or permanent residents only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:insideout-refocus-fund-postproduction-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123138Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inside Out RE:Focus Fund Post-Production Grants 2026
DESCRIPTION:Established in 2018 through a lead gift from Martha McCain\, In
 side Out's RE:Focus Fund addresses industry inequities by providing direct
  financial support to women\, non-binary\, and/or trans filmmakers telling
  2SLGBTQ+ stories. Launched in 2019\, the RE:Focus Fund Post-Production Gr
 ants support the post-production and promotion of short and feature films.
  Applications for the 2026 round open 29 June 2026. ELIGIBILITY: women\, n
 on-binary\, and/or trans filmmakers whose projects tell 2SLGBTQ+ stories\;
  per the FAQ\, international projects may be eligible to apply. The reques
 ted funding amount is determined by the applicant\, and unsuccessful appli
 cants can request feedback and reapply the following year. Inside Out also
  runs other RE:Focus initiatives\, including a new Residency program for f
 irst-time feature filmmakers facing intersectional barriers. KEY DATES: su
 bmissions open 29 June 2026\, close 22 July 2026\, with notifications by 1
 5 August 2026. Apply and read the FAQ via the Eventival page linked from t
 he RE:Focus Fund site\; questions to industry@insideout.ca.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Inside Out (Toronto 2SLGBTQ+ Film Festival)\nLocation: Toronto\, Canada
  (Inside Out is a Canadian organisation\; international projects may apply
 \, see FAQ)\nAward: Post-production grant funding to support the completio
 n and promotion of short and feature films\; the requested amount is set b
 y the applicant (see the FAQ 'How much funding can I request?'). Since 201
 8 the RE:Focus Fund has distributed more than CAD 300\,000 in grants and p
 rograms. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off post-production grant\n\nM
 ore: https://insideout.ca/initiatives/re-focus-fund/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://insideout.ca/initiatives/re-focus-fund/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Toronto\, Canada (Inside Out is a Canadian organisation\; internat
 ional projects may apply\, see FAQ)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
